#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# around_replace

#% text
# `around_replace(e, x -> around(m, s))` gives `e` at the uncertain value of `x`, the uncertainty propagated through `e` as a whole.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A list of rules gives several independent variables; `[x, y] -> vector_around(ms, c)` gives correlated ones.

#% item
# To first order the mean is `e` at the means and the variance `g . C . g`, `g` the gradient and `C` the covariance matrix. `around_replace(e, rules, 2)` works to second order: the mean adds `trace(H C)/2` and the variance `trace(H C H C)/2`, `H` the Hessian (the moments of normally distributed variables).

#% item
# Because `e` is taken as a whole, a variable occurring several times is correlated with itself, unlike in arithmetic on `around` values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
around_replace(exp(s) + exp(s/2), s -> around(2., 0.3))
#% output
#= around(10.107337927389695, 2.624459103948052)

#% cell
exp(around(2., 0.3)) + exp(around(2., 0.3)/2)
#% output
#= around(10.107337927389695, 2.2539048926715877)

#% cell
around_replace(x*y, [x -> around(2., 0.1), y -> around(3., 0.2)])
#% output
#= around(6.0, 0.5)

#% cell
around_replace(sin(s), s -> around(1.56, 0.01), 2)
#% output
#= around(0.9998917231439548, 0.00012905437448170262)

#% cell
around_replace(exp(s) + exp(r/2), [s, r] -> vector_around([2., 2.], [[0.3, 0.3], 0.5]))
#% output
#= around(10.107337927389695, 2.446208582020831)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `around`, `vector_around`
